Each analyzer emitted the enum CONTAINER and none of its members, so the
containment linker had nothing to root and a reverse slice from the enum
returned the container alone. Same defect WI-duguk drained for the eight
analyzers the G2 parity matrix gates; these three sit outside that matrix
by construction, so nothing caught it. scala.py's own comment recorded
the gap as deliberate: "Enum CASES ... remain out of scope".

Measured end-to-end, not from the analyzer tests:

  contains_out                  0 -> 3   (all three)
  slice --entry Color --reverse 1 -> 4   (all three)

Member naming follows each analyzer's OWN convention, because the
containment linker splits on the first separator it finds:
scala Color.Red, cpp Color::Red, kotlin Color.RED.

KOTLIN'S CONTAINER IS A CLASS, NOT AN ENUM. `enum class` is emitted as
kind="class" carrying an "enum" modifier; there is no kind="enum"
anywhere in that analyzer. The member loop therefore gates STRUCTURALLY
on the presence of an `enum_class_body` node rather than on the kind or
the modifier string. `class` is in both containment.CONTAINER_KINDS and
the slicer's set, so members root and expand with no kind change — but
anything keyed on the enum kind (including the parity matrix's own
_ENUM_CONTAINER_KINDS) will never see a Kotlin enum, which is why this
was verified by containment count and not by an enum-kind query.

TWO PER-LANGUAGE TRAPS, each of which would have shipped a partial fix:

  * scala has TWO member node types (simple_enum_case, full_enum_case),
    and `case Green, Blue` is ONE enum_case_definitions holding TWO
    siblings — so the walk iterates CASES, not groups. A per-group loop
    drops every case after a comma while leaving the container looking
    populated.
  * cpp's emission sits deliberately INSIDE the `if name_node:` guard.
    An anonymous `typedef enum { P, Q } Anon;` emits no container, so
    emitting its members would produce symbols whose dotted owner does
    not exist — orphans by construction, worse than the recall miss.

ONE EXISTING TEST CHANGED, and it was pinning the defect:
test_enum_body_val_emits_field asserted `_fields(syms) == {"Color.rgb"}`
over a fixture that has always contained `case Red`. The exact-set
assertion did not merely permit the missing case, it encoded it as the
expected answer — anyone fixing scala enum members would have seen this
go red and had to decide whether their fix was wrong.

Standing enforcement is a dedicated test file rather than a matrix cell:
the G2 matrix gates eight core-availability fixture languages and its
docstring names scala/kotlin/cpp as ungated, citing this item. 15 tests
across the three languages, including the comma-separated,
parameterised, anonymous and forward-declaration edge cases.

mypy delta +0 — pure recall improvement, no type-surface cost.
6886 tests pass, scoped coverage clean.

